Conventional STLF approaches often relate to statistical


models, such as the autoregressive moving average (ARMA)


[12], autoregressive integrated moving average (ARIMA) [13],


and exponential smoothing model [14]. These approaches are


straightforward to implement and interpretable, but their ability


to anticipate nonlinear series is low, so they cannot effec-


tively handle the STLF problem. In comparison to conventional


STLF approaches, machine learning has better nonlinear ﬁt-


ting capabilities. The back-propagation neural network [15] and


support vector regression (SVR) [16] are commonly employed


machine learning time-series prediction algorithms. The back-


propagation neural network has excellent parameter ﬁtting


skills, but owing to its simple structure, it has low generaliza-


tion abilities and is prone to the local optimum. As SVR is


based on the learning criterion of structural reduction, it has a


high degree of generalizability. SVR performs well with small


data sample sizes, but it fails to converge with high sample


sizes. Machine learning can learn nonlinear correlations between


sequences better than conventional methods, but it typically


has drawbacks including weak generalization capabilities, trou-


ble establishing hyper-parameters, and prediction accuracy that


frequently depends on the quality and quantity of input data.


Deep learning superimposes multilayer neural networks, which


may realize multilayer nonlinear mapping. With their nonlin-


ear mapping and adaptable capacities, systems based on deep


learning provide a viable solution for load forecasting [17–20].


Table 1 shows the related works on STLF. X. Kong [21] pro-


posed an improved deep belief network (DBN) method from


the three aspects of model structure, parameter optimization,


and data selection for better use in load forecasting. In [22], a


prediction method of a novel end-to-end neural network model


using deep residual neural networks (ResNets) was proposed.


Deep learning can abstract nonlinear interactions layer by


layer, which can increase the accuracy of STLF in comparison to


machine learning. However, a deep neural network (DNN), like


DBN and ResNet, is insensitive to temporal correlation, mak-


ing it difﬁcult to obtain better prediction outcomes. Considering


energy consumption is a typical time series, the model’s output


should relate to the past input. LSTM, a variant of the recurrent


neural network (RNN), is highly competitive in various ﬁelds


and can take the temporal dependencies of the time series. In


[23], LSTM was employed to predict loads for individual users,


and its superiority to traditional methods was demonstrated. In


consideration of the temporal features of the load data, LSTM


